at we always correctly stay  within 64 bits but usually you'll just  use begins like that and they'll grow as  necessary another value related feature  is computation with base ten floating  point numbers at the moment javascript  numbers are base two floating point  numbers and then you have the classic  here that sometimes used to to make fun  of javascript but actually that's an  issue that other languages have have  just as well so if you do the same  computation and say python you get the  same issue and because python as well as  javascript they're both the numbers are  based on the on I Triple E standards so  that's nothing JavaScript specific if on  the other hand you have base ten  floating-point numbers you don't get any  problems and the result is as you'd  expect and that matters whenever you do  scientific computing in JavaScript and  it also matters when you work in FinTech  and do any kind of financial computation  so if you do your taxes in JavaScript  you don't want there to be any rounding  errors  when it comes to categorizing values  javascript is also a tiny bit quirky you  have to decide if you want to use type  of instance of depending on the check  you want to perform a type of has its  own set of quirks typeof null is the the  famous one that null is the type of  things that now is an object which it  isn't instance of has the issue that if  a an instance or a class comes from  another realm like a frame in in a web  browser then instance of doesn't work as  you'd expect the next group of features  is about functional programming if you  look at languages that have syntax that  comes from the C family then they make  an unfortunate distinction between  expressions and statements so  expressions are syntactic constructs  that produce values when they're  evaluated and statements are constructs  that do something and that leads to  JavaScript having two kinds of  conditional constructs so on one hand  you have the the ternary operator which  i